title: "Introduction"
description: "Test software that reads the Stellar ledger against rare but valid transaction shapes, each tagged with the CAP that introduced it and emitted by a real stellar-core."
---

{/* Generated by apps/docs/scripts/generate.py from apps/docs/data/index.mdx.in and tracked data files; edit the template, not this file. */}

XDRCheck is for maintainers of Stellar SDKs and XDR libraries (js-stellar-sdk, go-stellar-sdk, rs-stellar-xdr,
py-stellar-base) and for authors and operators of indexers (wallet-backend, stellar-etl, Horizon, your own). It
tests your code against transaction shapes that are rare but valid: a muxed account, a signed-payload signer, a fee
bump around a failed transaction, a deeply nested authorization tree. Each shape is a corpus entry tagged with the
CAP that introduced it and, where stellar-core can emit it, produced by a real stellar-core.

After this page you know what the kit has checked, and which page to open for your role.

## What you can check

Each figure is generated from a tracked file and links to the page that says how it was made and how to run it
again.

- **Pubnet ledgers @@scan_first@@ to @@scan_last@@, every one, checked.** @@scan_ledgers@@ ledgers and
  @@scan_txs@@ transactions were read from the public data lake, decoded, re-encoded byte for byte and
  hash-checked: @@scan_refusals@@ ledgers refused and @@scan_mismatches@@ mismatches over @@scan_links@@ header
  hash-chain links. The scan computes shapes and these checks; no pipeline or SDK was replayed over all of it.
  [Mainnet scan](/results/mainnet-scan)
- **Entries a real stellar-core emitted.** @@entries@@ corpus entries, each tagged with its CAP and protocol;
  @@both_passphrases@@ of them were produced by a local, offline stellar-core under both the testnet and the mainnet
  passphrase, @@captured@@ were captured from network history and @@golden@@ come from stellar-core's own goldens.
  The @@synthetic@@ hand-made entries are marked synthetic. [How to read a corpus entry](/start/read-an-entry)
- **Public incidents reproduced before and after their fixes.** Three wallet-backend incidents and three
  js-stellar-sdk incidents fail on their pre-fix versions and pass on the fixed ones (`make verify-replay`,
  `make verify-conformance`). @@known_defects_reproduced@@ of the @@known_defects_total@@ public incidents on that page are
  reproduced this way; the others are run, checked or held as corpus input.
  [Known incidents](/results/incidents)
- **One corpus across the SDKs.** @@sdk_columns@@ columns (releases of the @@sdk_names@@ SDKs, and stellar-core)
  in one conformance matrix, so a defect found in one SDK is checked in the others.
  [SDK conformance matrix](/results/sdk-matrix)
- **Findings filed upstream.** @@findings_total@@ findings with a public record, @@findings_filed@@ of them filed
  upstream. [Findings](/results/findings)

