## What the kit is

| Part | What it does | How it works |
|---|---|---|
| Corpus | One entry per rare shape: the bytes, the CAP and protocol, and the values a correct reader must produce. Hand-made entries are marked `synthetic` | [The corpus](/architecture/corpus) |
| Generator | Runs scenarios on a local, offline stellar-core under the testnet and mainnet passphrases; imports stellar-core goldens and public-lake ledgers | [The corpus](/architecture/corpus#where-the-bytes-come-from) |
| Replay harness | Serves the corpus to your unmodified indexer as a SEP-54 data lake or a mock `getLedgers`, and compares what it stored with each entry | [Replay](/architecture/replay) |
| Service rigs and judge | Run real ledgers through wallet-backend, stellar-etl or Horizon at any commit; the judge derives what happened from each ledger's own entry changes | [Replay](/architecture/replay#real-ledgers-service-rigs-and-the-judge) |
| Conformance runner | Runs the corpus through the JavaScript, Go, Rust and Python SDKs and reports where they disagree with the bytes or with each other | [Conformance](/architecture/conformance) |
| `xdrlint` | Finds ingestion mistakes and assumptions about the ledger's form in Go code built on go-stellar-sdk | [Linter and guide](/architecture/linter-and-guide) |
| Hardening guide | Which fields stellar-core validates, which caps to set, where to take participants from | [Hardening guide](/hardening/overview) |
| Mainnet scan and `find` | A shape census of pubnet history; `xdrcheck find` turns a token, value tag or CAP into mainnet transactions to replay | [Mainnet scan](/architecture/mainnet-scan) |

Every part reads the same corpus manifest ([Manifest and index format](/reference/manifest)). The diagram of how
they connect is on [Architecture overview](/architecture/overview#how-the-parts-fit-together).

## Conventions on this site

- The command-line tools are `xdrcheck`, `xdrcheck-corpus`, `xdrlint`, `xdrcheck-judge` and `xdrcheck-tagger`
  ([Command-line reference](/reference/cli)).
- `OWNER/REPO` stands for the XDRCheck repository on GitHub, in URLs, `uses:` lines and Go module paths alike.
- Commands run from the root of a checkout of the repository unless a step says otherwise. After `make build`,
  the tools are in `./bin`; add them to your `PATH` with `export PATH="$PWD/bin:$PATH"`.
- A corpus release is named by the protocol it covers: `30.0.0` covers Protocol 30. See
  [Release and channels](/guides/maintain-kit#release-and-channels). XDRCheck is in active development toward its first release; until
  it is published, run everything from a checkout of the repository, and read `<version>` and `<commit>` in
  commands as the release or commit you pin.
- The figures on this site, the README's results and the dashboard (`apps/dashboard`) are generated from the same
  tracked files under `corpus/` and `evidence/`.
